#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153053} :activity/id #uuid "7d3eac14-632f-5a58-a023-44f29da9dd83" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049009 :topic/id #uuid "d31d3400-00a2-5388-a09e-ece03d04c735" :topic/math-academy-id 2623} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153053 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153352 :tutorial/content "Remember that the column space of a matrix is the span of its columns. For example, for the matrix\n\n![](images/6f/6f8184c584bac2ae9f4f0655d6de2f05bae48142caa729cc24faa30eb5e38774.svg)\n\nthe column space is\n\n![](images/83/83a4941812514b331349e96637ba4fc3d9686e85704506855f931efc3ed3d539.svg)\n\nSo, to find a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A),$ we can apply the usual procedure of converting the matrix $A$ to row echelon form and then picking the pivot columns from the original matrix $A.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "141672d1-fa24-513d-ba34-8cbf5a1d0b84" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3965 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "89804eda-a3ae-5485-b48e-d4ab4a5760e0" :step/math-academy-id 23536 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153054}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153054 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152155 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151943 :question/id #uuid "fc21ef44-25b7-5157-bbdb-c1284d1aa7ae" :question/math-academy-id "e-6254"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "39d79985-57fa-554a-b697-4bd6dd71cbe2" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049723}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152581} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152583} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247215} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247216} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318978} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318982} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345025} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423620}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis of the Column Space of a Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "675dd937-a474-5a45-ab38-929745f4d3a7" :step/math-academy-id 23537 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153055}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153055 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153353 :tutorial/content "In the previous example, we computed a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A),$ where \n\n![](images/c8/c8e10a79d647ed7867d42eae1eff7d37840f541cfce26e47d84f66da4e9f0c96.svg)\n\nThe basis that we found previously was\n\n![](images/84/84cdf6af82bd0d301ca50b1c7c41bccbd0a3cfb42be17bc84ec2f12e46032161.svg)\n\nTherefore, we can write\n\n![](images/22/22d4eb11af0168d4fabc8b3ebbe1befd24620db11b6a31709472ba91bb1ed794.svg)\n\nThe vector $\\mathbf{a}_3$ was not included in the basis because the $3$rd column of $A$ was not a pivot column. This means that $\\mathbf{a}_3$ can be written as a linear combination of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2.$\n\nNow, what if we want to find the exact linear combination of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ that yields $\\mathbf{a}_3?$ That is to say, we want to find $x_1, x_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}$ such that \n\n![](images/f3/f3f4e0d48c58be10684eada3fd320001dc056f2d1070707d9443c9533b7b5e35.svg)\n\nThis is equivalent to a system with the augmented matrix\n![](images/cf/cf66dc341420f6c399e1ad7e108986505cb349d3aba1aeaacee636e7b6ec4b94.svg)\n\nIf we reduced this matrix to the *reduced* row echelon form, we get\n\n![](images/84/847a4dd2e37e6e577bd9a38160f03bcb5839fbf617d76ebe0a2b42cdba0ebce1.svg)\n\nFinally, the third column of the *reduced* matrix tells us that $x_1=3$ and $x_2=-2,$ meaning that\n![](images/18/1892b80eb017795a7420b59053b836bea894ff77b7478fc7f884de6b43384cb1.svg)\n\nIn general, if the $n$th column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express $\\mathbf{a}_n$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. \n\n**Watch out!** The matrix must be written in *reduced* row echelon form (with $1$'s for pivots and $0$'s above and below the pivots), not just in a row echelon form." :tutorial/id #uuid "cb82414a-0cb2-5363-9301-c8d79896bc22" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3966 :tutorial/title "Finding a Particular Linear Combination of Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "3bd1dbe7-2b70-5718-9b1c-1634535fb285" :step/math-academy-id 23538 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153056}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153056 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152156 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151944 :question/id #uuid "302059c1-d807-54d2-badc-a4a19b2f9c05" :question/math-academy-id "e-6255"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "db27967e-f80c-587b-9b10-28f1a5518f57" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049422}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152579} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152580} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247221} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247222} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247223} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247225} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345026} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345138} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345139} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345143}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Given Set Is a Basis of the Column Space of a Matrix With Two Rows"} :step/id #uuid "665e0a4a-ccbf-5a7f-9823-1d0163d85f44" :step/math-academy-id 23539 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153057}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153057 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152157 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151945 :question/id #uuid "d5bcbec9-0b72-5ba1-ad84-c770cf3a0c0b" :question/math-academy-id "e-6272"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3bf9ea3-d57f-53f6-aa3c-eddffe1a8cfa" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050178}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152582} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152721} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247227} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247228} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247232} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247233} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345149} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345151} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423621}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Given Set Is a Basis of the Column Space of a Matrix For a Larger Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "4fcbb627-ddcb-5a6f-aa6f-8950fb6d730a" :step/math-academy-id 23569}] :activity/title "Finding a Basis of the Column Space of a Matrix" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186152330}]}
